{"passage": "In recent years, technology has changed the way people live and work, and it has done so faster than most of us expected. Many families now use smart devices at home, and these tools can help with simple daily tasks such as turning off lights or reminding us to drink water. However, when we depend too much on machines, we may slowly lose some basic skills that our parents learned when they were young. Researchers who study human behaviour have found that people often trust a machine's advice more than their own judgement, especially when the machine speaks in a confident voice. This is a problem worth thinking about, because a device can be wrong even when it sounds sure of itself.

One clear example is the way students use technology to learn. A student can now search for information in seconds, but finding an answer quickly does not always mean understanding it deeply. When learners copy facts without asking why those facts are true, their knowledge stays shallow and disappears soon after the test. Teachers have noticed that classes which discuss ideas together produce stronger thinking than classes which simply watch videos, although videos can still be useful for showing things that are hard to imagine. The best results seem to come when a teacher guides the discussion and technology supports it.

