{"passage":"Technology has changed the way students learn, and the classroom of today looks very different from the one their parents knew, because digital tools now sit beside books and paper on almost every desk. A student who once waited days for a library book can now reach a huge amount of information in seconds, and this easy access has given young people an ability to study that earlier generations could hardly imagine. Teachers, however, are careful to point out that a fast answer is not always a good answer, and they often ask learners to check several sources before they accept what a screen tells them.

One clear advantage of modern technology is that it can adapt to each learner. A student who finds a topic difficult can watch a short video again at home, while a classmate who already understands it can move on to a harder task, so the same lesson can serve very different needs. This flexibility is valuable, yet it also places a new duty on the young, since nobody is standing beside them to keep their attention on the work. Many parents worry about this, and some of them set simple rules about when phones may be used and when they must be put away.

Another benefit is the way technology connects people across distance. A volunteer in a small town can join an online discussion with experts far away, and a teacher can invite a guest speaker into the room without any travel at all. Such experiences widen the view of everyone involved, although they cannot replace the warmth of a real conversation. The most sensible approach, therefore, is to treat technology as a powerful tool rather than a master, using it with care while keeping the human side of learning alive."}

{"passage":"Technology has changed the way people live, work and learn, and it has done so with a speed that few could have predicted. A student who once carried heavy books to class can now store an entire library on a small device, which means that information is no longer limited to those who can afford to buy many books. This change is not simply convenient; it also gives people the ability to learn about almost any subject whenever they wish.

However, the same technology that brings such benefits can also create problems that are easy to ignore. When a person spends hours looking at a screen, sleep and health may suffer, and the ability to focus on one task for a long time can slowly weaken. A teacher may notice that students who use devices constantly find it harder to remember facts, because they know that a machine will always provide the answer.

What matters most is not the tool itself but the way people choose to use it. A volunteer who teaches older neighbours to use a smartphone can help them stay in touch with family, while a parent who sets clear limits at home can protect a child from harmful content. In this sense, technology is neither good nor bad; its effect depends on the choices that people make every day."}

{"passage":"Technology has changed the way people live, work, and communicate, and it has become an important part of daily life for almost everyone. Although many people welcome these changes, others worry that the fast development of new tools may create problems that are difficult to solve. When a student uses a smartphone to look up information for a project, the ability to find answers quickly can save a great deal of time and help the student focus on deeper thinking instead of simple memory work. However, the same device may also distract the student from study if it is used without care, so balance is essential.

In many classrooms, teachers now use digital tools to explain difficult ideas, and this approach allows students to see pictures, videos, and models that make abstract ideas easier to understand. Because technology can support learning in this way, schools are spending more money on equipment and training, which helps both teachers and students adapt to a changing world. Some parents, however, remain anxious about screen time and ask whether young people spend too little time reading books or playing outside.

The volunteer in a community centre offers free lessons on how to use the internet safely, and the neighbour who attends these lessons says the advice has helped her avoid false messages online. Since technology will continue to develop, people need to learn how to use it wisely, and the most successful users are those who stay curious while also thinking carefully about the risks."}

