{"passage": "Artificial intelligence has become a normal part of daily life, and it quietly shapes the choices that ordinary people make from morning to night. When a student opens a phone to check the weather, an intelligent system has already studied vast amounts of data and produced a forecast that is often surprisingly accurate. This same technology helps a doctor recognise an illness earlier, allows a farmer to water crops more efficiently, and enables a traveller to find a faster route through a crowded city. Such abilities sound almost magical, yet they arise from simple mathematics combined with enormous computing power.

However, the rapid advance of AI also raises serious questions that society cannot afford to ignore. Because machines can now write essays, create pictures, and even hold conversations, many workers worry that their jobs may disappear within a decade. A teacher may wonder whether students are learning genuinely or merely copying answers generated by a clever programme. A parent might feel uneasy about how much personal information a child's device collects every day. These concerns are reasonable, and they deserve careful public discussion rather than blind enthusiasm.

Fortunately, people are not powerless in this situation. Governments are beginning to pass laws that require companies to explain how their systems reach decisions, and schools are teaching young people to question what a machine tells them. If citizens stay curious and demand transparency, technology can remain a tool that serves human needs instead of a force that controls them. The future of AI will therefore depend less on the intelligence of machines than on the wisdom of the people who design and use them."}
